%N A334504 Eventual period of a single cell in rule 26 cellular automaton in a cyclic universe of width n.
%C A334504 _Bradley Klee_ computed a(1)-a(10).
%C A334504 For odd n >= 3, the orbit is purely periodic: at time t, cell i is alive iff s = (i+t) mod n is even and binomial(t, s/2) is odd, i.e., a window of Pascal's triangle mod 2 drifting around the ring. For even n = 2m >= 4, from time 1 on the orbit is a mirror-symmetric copy of rule 90 on m-1 cells with null boundaries started from an end cell, which is the system computed in A268754. - _Chi Hsia_, Nov 22 2026
%D A334504 Bradley Klee, Posting to Math Fun Mailing List, Apr 26 2020
%H A334504 Bert Dobbelaere, <a href="/A334504/b334504.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..120</a>
%H A334504 Chi Hsia, <a href="https://github.com/Bonnie9981/Project-Discovery/blob/main/paper/a334504.pdf">Proof of the formulas for a(2n+1) and a(2n+2)</a>, 2026. Proof and Lean 4 formalization generated with an LLM (Claude) and machine-checked; see the acknowledgements.
%F A334504 It seems that a(2*n+2) = A268754(n) and a(2*n+1) = (2*n+1) * 2^A070939(n) = A363121(n+1)/2 for n > 0. - _Andrei Zabolotskii_, Sep 04 2024
%F A334504 The two formulas conjectured above, a(2*n+1) = (2*n+1) * 2^A070939(n) and a(2*n+2) = A268754(n) for n > 0, are true; see the proof in the links. - _Chi Hsia_, Nov 22 2026
